    {"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"The article contrasts two ways of reading philosophy's past. Which best describes 'contextualism' (associated with Quentin Skinner)?","options":["Extract a past philosopher's argument, formalize it, and assess it as a contribution to our current problems","Read each text as answering the questions of its OWN moment, in the vocabulary of its own debates","Treat the history of philosophy as a record of superseded errors","Begin the story of philosophy only with the Greeks"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Contextualism insists a text intervenes in the debates of its time and must be read in its own vocabulary — reading Locke as addressing modern liberalism would be 'ventriloquism.' Its opposite, 'rational reconstruction,' mines past arguments for present use. The article argues mature scholarship toggles between both lenses."},
    {"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Socrates' distinctive contribution to philosophy was primarily:","options":["A complete system of metaphysics","A method — the elenchus, cross-examination that exposes that confident experts cannot define what they claim to know","The theory of Forms","Formal logic"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Socrates left doctrines to others; his legacy is a method of relentless questioning (the elenchus) and the self-image of philosophy as gadfly. The theory of Forms is Plato's; formal logic is Aristotle's."},
    {"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"The Buddha's philosophically radical thesis, as the article presents it, is that:","options":["The material world is an illusion","There is no enduring self (anatman) — only causally linked processes","Only brahman is ultimately real","Suffering can never be diagnosed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The Buddha's 'no-self' (anatman) doctrine holds there is no permanent self, only causally connected processes, and that craving built on the mistaken idea of a self is the root of suffering. This launched two millennia of TECHNICAL debate (Nyaya logicians, Nagarjuna, Dignaga) — not mysticism. ('Only brahman is real' is Shankara's Vedanta, a different school.)"},
    {"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Kant's 'Copernican revolution' in philosophy was the proposal that:","options":["The Earth goes around the Sun","Instead of asking how the mind conforms to objects, we ask how objects must conform to the mind's own forms (space, time, the categories)","All knowledge comes from sense experience alone","Metaphysics can prove the existence of God, the soul, and the world as a whole"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Kant reversed the direction of fit: the mind contributes the forms (space, time, causality) that any possible experience must take, so mathematics and physics gain necessity WITHIN experience — while old-style metaphysics of God/soul/world oversteps the bounds of cognition and collapses into antinomies."},
    {"id":"q5","type":"open","prompt":"The article opens by claiming philosophy 'cannot escape its history' — that living philosophers argue WITH Plato, Hume, and Kant. Give the article's structural (non-embarrassing) explanation for this, and name the two opposed methods for reading the history that follow from it.","rubric":"The embarrassing explanation is that philosophy never settles anything; the better, structural one is that philosophy's core questions (what exists, what can be known, how to live, what is just) are such that every systematic ANSWER changes what the question means — so the history is not a graveyard of errors but the CURRENT STATE OF THE ARGUMENT, and you cannot enter the argument without knowing how the positions got their shapes. The two reading methods: (1) RATIONAL RECONSTRUCTION — treat past philosophers as colleagues contributing to our problems, extract and assess their arguments independent of their era; and (2) CONTEXTUALISM (Skinner / the Cambridge school) — read each text as answering the questions of its own moment in its own vocabulary. The article argues both are partly right and mature work toggles between them."},
    {"id":"q6","type":"open","prompt":"Why does the article insist on treating Indian and Chinese philosophy as philosophy 'full stop,' and what comparative payoff does setting them beside Greece give the historian?","rubric":"The Thales-to-Wittgenstein, all-European-men canon is a NINETEENTH-CENTURY construction (earlier histories began philosophy in Egypt, Persia, or India); the omission of Indian and Chinese thought was a decision, not a discovery. Those traditions are rigorous, argumentative, and self-critical — e.g., India's Nyaya logicians and epistemology of knowledge-sources, Nagarjuna's arguments, the Vedanta debates; China's Confucian, Mohist, Daoist, and Legalist argument over the Way and how to govern. The comparative payoff: because these traditions developed largely independently, seeing which problems RECUR everywhere (the skeptic's challenge, realism about universals, cultivation vs rule-following) versus which are LOCAL is evidence about which philosophical problems belong to reason as such and which are creatures of particular languages and social forms."}
  ]$hp_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'philosophy/history-of-philosophy', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
